## When surgery is offered

In the early stages, stronger glasses and brighter lighting may be enough. NICE says surgery should be offered when a cataract is **affecting your daily life**, not based on how cloudy it is.

## What happens

- it is usually done as a **day case** under **local anaesthetic**, so you are awake but cannot feel it
- the surgeon removes the cloudy lens and replaces it with a clear, artificial lens
- it usually takes about **15 to 30 minutes**
- if both eyes need surgery, they are usually done on different days

## Recovery

- you will use **eye drops** for a few weeks
- avoid rubbing your eye, swimming and heavy lifting for a short time
- most people notice better sight within a few days

## Possible problems

Complications are uncommon. Contact the eye clinic urgently if your sight gets worse, or your eye becomes very red or painful, after surgery.

Months or years later, the capsule behind the lens can become cloudy (**posterior capsule opacification**). This is treated with a quick, painless laser.
